Comment: 
"// @@@ For every valid index i (from 0 up to the size of the list), if the element at index i in the list is null, then the corresponding element in the resulting array is also null; otherwise, the element in the resulting array is equal to the element in the list at index i."

We are inside toArray method. The method returns Object[] array. We need to assert postcondition about the returned array relative to the list.

We can use \result to refer to return value. We need to express for all int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); then (this.get(i) == null => \result[i] == null) && (this.get(i) != null => \result[i].equals(this.get(i))) . Since list may contain null, we need equality.

In JML style: assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null ? \result[i] == null : \result[i].equals(this.get(i)));

But they used earlier pattern: \forall int i; 0<=i && i<\old(this.getCount()); this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i)));

Here we need condition with null check. Could use implication: (this.get(i) == null => \result[i] == null) && (this.get(i) != null => \result[i].equals(this.get(i))). Combine with && inside forall.

Thus final assert:

assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null => \result[i] == null) && (this.get(i) != null => \result[i].equals(this.get(i)));

